Choose execution from extracted dependencies. Execute only coupled steps in the main session. When the plan declares independent units, dispatch ready units concurrently to sub-agents; when the plan does not declare units but ships independent ready steps with real dependencies and exact authorized ownership, treat them as the same ready group and dispatch them too. Respect dependencies, collect each unit's implementation and verification proof, then integrate and run cross-unit and full verification in the main session. Never let two agents edit the same file; keep integration, final verification, and artifact submission in the main session.

Use only the orchestration tools actually exposed by the running runtime. A full dispatch slot means a queued wave plus useful local work on the next safe increment, never idle abandonment. When no native sub-agent / task tool is exposed by the runtime — or the available one is unavailable for this attempt — fall back to bounded sequential progress on the same dependency graph: pick the next ready reference, implement it, verify it, and continue; do not invent tool calls or produce a size-only refusal. When the plan declares units the runtime cannot dispatch (cap exhausted, ownership unresolvable, malformed graph), keep that work in the main session rather than broadening scope.

Ownership may come from a unit's exact `Paths:`, its `Directories:` (the declared directory limits), or its steps' `Files:` when the unit has neither. Do not broaden file ownership to a parent directory. Remove assignments to `.agent`, `.git`, and `.worktrees` (and descendants) before making a worker brief. An unknown or empty effective scope, malformed dependency graph, or unextractable ownership stays in the main session; it never grants broad worker writes.

A worker cap limits concurrent units, not total units: queue later ready units in waves. Serialize conflicting ownership—equal files, directory ancestry, and directory/file containment conflict—until the earlier writer finishes. Units with disjoint file ownership in one directory may proceed together. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency.

Before transferring ownership from one writer to another (a new worker, a fallback, or a re-dispatch), confirm the former writer has actually stopped — the assignment is closed, the result is persisted, and no new work is in flight on the same path. Never overlap writers on the same file. Release a dependent step only after the upstream writer's evidence is validated, not on a self-declared completion.

For every dispatched unit, give the worker its unit ID, assigned steps, exact effective ownership, focused verification, and the brokered-tools-only rule. Workers do not commit, integrate, or submit the main artifact. Their result is a lead the main session checks before final reconciliation. Raw plan prose remains authoritative: retain all residual prose work and unowned global steps in the main session, even when extraction cannot represent them as worker assignments.
